{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","content":"Exit code 1\nCLAUDE.md\nDockerfile\nLICENSE\nMakefile\nREADME.md\ncmd\ndeploy\ndocs\ngo.mod\ngo.sum\ninternal\n.ard/243:\ncosts\nlogs\ntriage-lead.md\n\ndeploy:\nargocd\ncharts\ncosign.pub\ntest-charts.sh\ntest-image.sh\ntest-repo.sh\ntestdata\nVERSION ?= $(shell git describe --tags --always --dirty 2>/dev/null || echo dev)\nLDFLAGS := -s -w -X main.version=$(VERSION)\n\n.PHONY: build test lint fmt clean cve-fixtures lint-scanner-fixtures chart-test image-test\n\nbuild:\n\tCGO_ENABLED=0 go build -ldflags \"$(LDFLAGS)\" -o bin/ard ./cmd/ard\n\ntest:\n\tgo test ./...\n\nlint:\n\tgo vet ./...\n\t@out=\"$$(gofmt -l .)\"; if [ -n \"$$out\" ]; then echo \"gofmt needed:\"; echo \"$$out\"; exit 1; fi\n\nfmt:\n\tgofmt -w .\n\n# Renders the two Argo CD charts under deploy/ and checks them (needs helm and yq;\n# run by the ard-ci pipeline, not part of `make test`).\nchart-test:\n\tdeploy/test-charts.sh\n\n# Builds the ard image for both platforms and checks it (needs Docker buildx and\n# network; not part of `make test`).\nimage-test:\n\tdeploy/test-image.sh\n\n# Regenerates the real cve scanner fixtures inside the pinned tool image.\n# Needs Docker and network; not part of `make test`.\ncve-fixtures:\n\tdocker build -t ard-cve-fixtures internal/roles/scanners/cve\n\tdocker run --rm --user \"$$(id -u):$$(id -g)\" -e HOME=/tmp \\\n\t  -v \"$$PWD/internal/roles:/roles\" -w /roles ard-cve-fixtures sh testdata/cve/regen.sh\n\n# Regenerates the real lint scanner fixtures in the pinned image (see\n# internal/roles/testdata/lint/regen.sh). Needs Docker; not part of `make test`.\nlint-scanner-fixtures:\n\tdocker build -t ard-lint-fixtures internal/roles/scanners/lint\n\tdocker run --rm --user \"$$(id -u):$$(id -g)\" -e HOME=/tmp \\\n\t  -v \"$$PWD/internal/roles:/roles\" -w /roles ard-lint-fixtures sh testdata/lint/regen.sh\n\nclean:\n\trm -rf bin\n(eval):1: no matches found: --include=*.go\n12-  # built-in list: removing an entry removes its gate. An entry ending in /\n13-  # is a directory prefix; anything else is a glob (quote globs starting with *).\n14:  protected_paths:\n15-    - .github/\n16-    - .ard/config.yaml\n17-    - .ard/scanners/\n18-    - .ard/triage/\n19-    - .ard/workers/\n20-    - .ard/reviewers/\n21-    - \"**/CODEOWNERS\"\n22-    # Agent instructions a PR could plant to steer later agents.\n23-    - CLAUDE.md\n24-    - \"**/CLAUDE.md\"\n25-    - AGENTS.md\n26-    - \"**/AGENTS.md\"\n1:# ard: Automated Repository Development\n5:## Summary\n22:## Architecture\n44:## Stack\n48:## Label state machine\n95:## The .ard/ contract\n97:`.ard/<issue#>/` is the only place agents leave durable output, and every file in it has one owner role. Issue directories are numeric, so they never collide with the role directories under `.ard/` (see Configuration).\n124:| a custom triage role | its role file only, unless `config.yaml` gives it `plan_section: <heading>` (see Configuration) |\n152:## Open issues\n166:## Scanners\n233:## Triage loop\n274:## Executors and the Reaper\n278:Local-executor details: the role directory (and its `Dockerfile`) is mounted from the config snapshot at the job's SHA, not from the working tree. The job worktree is always detached at a SHA resolved from the job's ref (the executor never checks out a branch; the committer owns branch worktrees). The controller's clone is a bare repository ard owns at `~/.ard/repos/<owner>/<repo>.git` (lowercased), with no remote, no stored credential and auto-gc off (`gc.auto=0`, `maintenance.auto=false`). `ard serve` creates it on first start and seeds it with one fetch of `main` (up to 30 min; if that fails, serve starts paused and the next start retries), and later starts reuse it. Config loads and committer lanes fetch into it, and every committer and executor worktree is cut from it (lanes under `~/.ard/work/<owner>/<repo>/lanes/`). `ard serve` never writes to the operator's checkout: it reads it only for the `origin` fallback of `--repo` and the local `paused` key. To rebuild a damaged clone, stop serve, `rm -rf` it and restart. Upgrading from a version that used the checkout: ard deletes its old lane and job worktree directories without running git there, so the old `refs/ard/*` refs and the dangling worktree registrations stay in the checkout until you remove them (`git for-each-ref --format='delete %(refname)' refs/ard/ | git update-ref --stdin; git worktree prune`). Every `git worktree add`, `remove` and `prune` on the controller's clone, by the executor and the committer's lanes alike, takes one in-process lock keyed by the clone's common git directory, because git's worktree bookkeeping is not safe under concurrent admin commands; fetches, commits and pushes do not take it. Each such command also has a 30 s deadline, and cleanup runs even when the job's context is cancelled, so one hung git cannot stall every lane. Do not run `git worktree prune` (or add/remove) in `~/.ard/repos/<owner>/<repo>.git` by hand while `ard serve` runs: the lock is per process and cannot cover a human's git. The operator's own checkout is free to use. The host keeps two files per job: `<base>.log` (stdout and\n\n... [2507 characters truncated] ...\n\nas no `Dockerfile`, and the `ard` image carries its pinned tools. Only deterministic scanners run in that pod: it starts `ard serve --loops scanners,stale,reaper` ([#187](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/issues/187)), so the triage, worker and reviewer loops never run there, even though `.ard/config.yaml` on `main` has agent roles. `--loops` takes a comma-separated list of loop names (scanners, stale, triage, workers, reviewers, reviewfix, merger, janitor, reaper, budget; `budget` is the status-issue notice loop, the claim gate is always on), defaults to every loop, and rejects an unknown name with exit 2. `ard serve` and `ard config check` refuse to run on git older than 2.47.0, because the committer's fetches fail on older versions.\n314:## Worker loop\n334:## Reviewer loop\n367:## Merger loop\n392:## Configuration\n394:Configuration is by convention: a role exists because its directory exists, and `.ard/config.yaml` holds only global settings and overrides. No prompt path is written anywhere; ard reads `.ard/<type>/<name>/prompt.txt`. Model credentials are host settings (`ARD_AGENT_AUTH`, `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`, `CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN` in the `ard serve` environment), not `config.yaml` keys, because a PR can change `config.yaml`.\n451:## Guardrails\n469:- **Kill switch.** Four sources stop new claims, scanner runs (filing and autoclose), stale closes and merges: a local pause file `~/.ard/pause/<owner>/<repo>` outside the repository (the path is lowercased; `ard serve` creates the directory, so `touch` pauses and `rm` resumes), `paused: true` in the `config.yaml` in the host's working tree, `ard:pause` on the pinned status issue, and `paused: true` in the config on `origin/main`. They are checked cheapest first, before every claiming tick. The delay is at most one loop interval plus 10% jitter; a tick already running may finish its claims. `SIGUSR1` (Unix only) makes every loop run one tick now, logged as `controller.wake`; the tick still passes the kill switch and the job pool, and wakes that arrive while a tick runs coalesce into one extra tick. The gate answers one controller-wide question and has no per-kind checks, so every loop that writes (scanner filing and autoclose, stale closes, merges, claims), except the Reaper, the cost-table writer and in-flight close-outs, must report `Claims() == true` and is skipped while paused. Running jobs, in-flight close-outs, the Reaper and the `costs` loop continue: the table is bookkeeping about work already done, not new work. The check fails closed: an unreadable status issue, a failed fetch of `origin/main`, or a config that fails to load counts as paused. The pause file pauses when anything exists at the path (a file, a directory or a symlink; content is ignored), and a path that cannot be examined, for example because `pause/<owner>` is a regular file, also counts as paused. The local working-tree `config.yaml` is read only for its `paused` key; a missing, unreadable or invalid file does not pause by itself, but a readable one that says `paused: true` does. The pause file and the local `paused` are the kill switches that nothing pushed to GitHub can lift. Config and role directories otherwise come from `origin/main` at the same SHA the tick's jobs use (see Configuration). `ard serve` remembers the status issue number in `_controller.db` (`meta` key `status_issue`). At startup it reads that issue directly and adopts it if it is still open, titled `ard status`, carries the `<!-- ard:status -->` marker and has the App as author; otherwise it searches by those same tests, and creates and pins the issue if none matches. Search is eventually consistent, so the remembered number is what stops a quick restart from creating a second issue; if two still match, the lowest number wins. A cached issue that cannot be read for a reason other than not-found falls back to search rather than aborting startup. A dry-run never stores its synthetic (negative) number; a real number found by search is stored only in the separate dry-run database. A second `ard serve` on the same repo exits with an error: the leader lock is an `flock` on `~/.ard/locks/<owner>/<repo>.lock` (local filesystem only), shared by live and dry-run.\n472:## Gaps in the original sketch\n492:## Delivery\n502:## Rollout\n517:**Where it runs (decided, #5; amended for the soak, [#144](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/issues/144)).** `ard serve` runs live on the owner's Mac with the local executor until Phase 3, when it moves to the \"spark\" Kubernetes cluster with the k8s executor and a Lease lock in place of the `flock`. The Gate 1 soak week does not run on the Mac: it runs on spark as `ard serve --dry-run` (one pod, `Recreate`, state on a persistent volume), and the week counts from the first healthy pod; no other `ard serve` for the same repo runs meanwhile, so one ledger is counted. See Delivery. ard supports macOS and Linux only; Windows is unsupported.\n529:## Open questions\nls: .ard/55: No such file or directory","is_error":true,"tool_use_id":"toolu_01MMTCbBPHHLC7VWeSPuUetU"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6629f6eb-a30e-4bf7-a39c-365e453abc49","uuid":"a51d52cf-e55a-49f3-9db6-fe9b2451aabb","timestamp":"2026-11-23T23:13:01.139Z","tool_use_result":"Error: Exit code 1\nCLAUDE.md\nDockerfile\nLICENSE\nMakefile\nREADME.md\ncmd\ndeploy\ndocs\ngo.mod\ngo.sum\ninternal\n.ard/243:\ncosts\nlogs\ntriage-lead.md\n\ndeploy:\nargocd\ncharts\ncosign.pub\ntest-charts.sh\ntest-image.sh\ntest-repo.sh\ntestdata\nVERSION ?= $(shell git describe --tags --always --dirty 2>/dev/null || echo dev)\nLDFLAGS := -s -w -X main.version=$(VERSION)\n\n.PHONY: build test lint fmt clean cve-fixtures lint-scanner-fixtures chart-test image-test\n\nbuild:\n\tCGO_ENABLED=0 go build -ldflags \"$(LDFLAGS)\" -o bin/ard ./cmd/ard\n\ntest:\n\tgo test ./...\n\nlint:\n\tgo vet ./...\n\t@out=\"$$(gofmt -l .)\"; if [ -n \"$$out\" ]; then echo \"gofmt needed:\"; echo \"$$out\"; exit 1; fi\n\nfmt:\n\tgofmt -w .\n\n# Renders the two Argo CD charts under deploy/ and checks them (needs helm and yq;\n# run by the ard-ci pipeline, not part of `make test`).\nchart-test:\n\tdeploy/test-charts.sh\n\n# Builds the ard image for both platforms and checks it (needs Docker buildx and\n# network; not part of `make test`).\nimage-test:\n\tdeploy/test-image.sh\n\n# Regenerates the real cve scanner fixtures inside the pinned tool image.\n# Needs Docker and network; not part of `make test`.\ncve-fixtures:\n\tdocker build -t ard-cve-fixtures internal/roles/scanners/cve\n\tdocker run --rm --user \"$$(id -u):$$(id -g)\" -e HOME=/tmp \\\n\t  -v \"$$PWD/internal/roles:/roles\" -w /roles ard-cve-fixtures sh testdata/cve/regen.sh\n\n# Regenerates the real lint s